<p>The United Arab Emirates said Tuesday it will leave OPEC and the broader OPEC+ alliance effective May 1, ending nearly six decades in the oil producers’ group and removing one of its largest members from coordinated production decisions. The state-run WAM news agency announced the decision after what the UAE described as a review of production policy, current and future capacity, and the national interest. The UAE said the move reflected its “long-term strategic and economic vision and evolving energy profile.” Energy Minister Suhail Mohamed al-Mazrouei told Reuters the decision followed a careful review of current and future policies related to production levels. The UAE has previously objected to OPEC production quotas it viewed as limiting its exports, and several accounts linked the decision to Abu Dhabi’s push for greater flexibility over output. The announcement came during severe disruption to Gulf energy exports tied to the Iran war and shipping risks in the Strait of Hormuz, a route between Iran and Oman that normally carries about one-fifth of the world’s crude oil and liquefied natural gas. Mazrouei said the departure would not have a huge market impact because Gulf producers already faced constraints in the strait.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/e-KZ7TAYNg5ENQQECxdwNbHSzhzaZdo07O_Ba_IUSHvN9-aGWHgLuKZfxeoiSzu42uKPv1uQY5MUbk6BPS9k431V1JkagOOZlUKKPDUwV2DYUQ36SxgsWZMSR0KmKNUKdTz6G2M5VPzlHYGK3yz3oCdgPZVUK54KkSllYAqngCUYl9yHrA' alt='An Emirati man stands at the oil terminal of Fujairah during the inauguration ceremony of a dock for supertankers on September 21, 2016.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Vienna timing: The WAM statement came as OPEC was preparing to meet in Vienna on Wednesday.</li><li>Saudi ties: Mazrouei said the UAE did not raise the withdrawal with Saudi Arabia, OPEC’s de facto leader, before announcing the decision.</li><li>Remaining members: After the UAE exit, OPEC will have 11 member countries, including Saudi Arabia, Iran and Iraq.</li></ul><blockquote>We thank OPEC and its member countries for decades of constructive cooperation - Suhail Mohamed al-Mazrouei</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>UAE Energy Ministry: The ministry said medium- to long-term trends pointed to sustained growth in global energy demand, despite near-term volatility. (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330457580/uae-leaves-opec-and-opec-in-huge-blow-to-group'>WAM via Aaj News</a>)</li><li>Reuters factbox: Reuters said the UAE has pursued an assertive foreign policy and carved out its own sphere of influence across the Middle East and Africa. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/factbox-uaes-foreign-policy-spotlight-after-opec-exit'>Reuters via Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>President Trump: President Trump has accused OPEC of inflating oil prices and linked U.S. military support for Gulf states to his criticism that producers impose high prices. (<a href='https://www.middleeasteye.net/live-blog/live-blog-update/uae-quit-opec-and-opec'>Middle East Eye</a>)</li><li>Semafor analysis: Semafor argued that alternative Gulf export routes may be useful but are likely uneconomic and at least a decade from completion. It also said the UAE’s diversified economy gives it a different long-term calculus from some Gulf producers. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/28/2026/uaes-saudi-schism-deepens-with-move-to-quit-opec'>Semafor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/apr/28/uae-quit-opec-oil-exporters-cartel-donald-trump'>UAE quits Opec in win for Trump as oil cartel weakened</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/uae-pulls-out-opec-oil-cartels-citing-national-interests'>UAE pulls out of OPEC oil cartels citing &#x27;national interests&#x27;</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/28/uae-to-withdraw-from-opec-and-opec_6752927_4.html'>UAE to withdraw f